.TH DATAPUMP-FB 1 "September 2026" "Data Pump 001_00" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.SH NAME
datapump-fb \- software framebuffer audio modem interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B datapump-fb
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The framebuffer interface shares Data Pump's application controls, message
policies, modem workers and plots. A CPU renderer draws the entire interface
into an owned RGBA framebuffer. The SDL2 host displays that buffer through a
software window surface and translates keyboard and mouse events.
It does not use FLTK, Rev, OpenGL or an SDL 3D renderer.
.PP
The framebuffer uses independent pixel widgets and the shared desktop/document
geometry, separate from the terminal layout. Small viewports can pan while
keyboard focus reveals controls. Tab and Shift+Tab change focus; arrows navigate; Enter activates or commits;
Escape dismisses a popup; F1 opens keyboard help; Ctrl+Q closes the application.
Editable fields accept typed values as well as offered presets. Mouse clicks
focus and activate controls; dragging selects editor text; the wheel scrolls.
Shift+wheel pans a small viewport horizontally. Ctrl+V pastes clipboard text
as data without activating commands.
.PP
The bundled font displays printable ASCII. Unsupported local Unicode glyphs
appear as question marks while the original editor bytes remain intact.
Received text retains Data Pump's stricter shared received-byte policy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-headless
Run without initializing SDL video. By default, produce one rendering iteration
and close. With \-\-smoke-test, run the complete shared application smoke workload.
.TP
.BI \-\-capture " FILE.ppm"
Write the final framebuffer as a binary RGB PPM image. An existing file is replaced.
.TP
.BI \-\-frames " N"
Close after N event-loop rendering iterations. A stable scene may reuse its
previous framebuffer; modem polling remains independent of drawing.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " WIDTHxHEIGHT"
Initial framebuffer dimensions, default 1200x1048. Each dimension is limited
to 8192 and the entire surface to 16 megapixels.
.TP
.BI \-\-font-scale " N"
Integer bitmap font scale from 1 through 4, default 2.
.TP
.B \-\-simulation
Use the shared simulated Robust Modem session instead of ordinary live audio.
.TP
.B \-\-color, \-\-monochrome
Select shared color or grayscale presentation.
.TP
.B \-\-self-check, \-\-smoke-test
Run the shared display-free check or full application workflow smoke.
The shared link-setting and smoke options described in datapump-gui(1) also apply.
.SH EMBEDDING
The datapump_framebuffer library and framebuffer.hpp expose a host-owned Runtime.
Construct one Runtime and call update(width, height, events) at least every
40 milliseconds. Each call returns one complete, immutable framebuffer containing
all widgets, menus, dialogs and plots. Upload it as a single texture or copy it
to a display. No per-widget callbacks or multiple render targets are required.
Optional clipboard and folder services are declined with an in-frame notice
when using this minimal interface. Advanced hosts can use tick and explicit
service methods instead. Width, height, stride, byte
format, revision and damage rectangles are explicit. Frames remain valid across
later rendering, resizing and runtime destruction.
.PP
Output uses RGBA8888 bytes; copy_frame also converts to BGRA8888 or RGB565
little-endian surfaces with caller-supplied strides. Damage is relative to the
immediately preceding frame revision. A host that skips a revision copies the
whole frame. Calls on a Runtime must be serialized by the host.
.PP
SDL belongs only to the executable host; the library has no window-system
dependency. A game-engine host can upload the frame as a texture and translate
ray intersections into pointer coordinates. This interface does not implement
a VR engine, physical Linux framebuffer driver, or microcontroller modem port.
.SH SEE ALSO
